WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.430
It’s been 20 years since Seinfeld left TV,
and its ratings-grabbing final episode remains

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.040
divisive among both critics and fans.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.360
Here's what happened during the last episode
of this long-running sitcom, and how it fits

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.860
into the rest of the hug-free, learning-free,
laugh-filled show about nothing.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.900
“So everybody I know is a character on the
show?”

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.400
“That’s right.”

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:22.420
“And it’s about nothing?”

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.360
“Absolutely nothing!”

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:24.940
Breaking the mold

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:30.060
While sitcoms existed before Seinfeld, no
other show captured the quirks of everyday

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.220
life quite like it.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:37.380
Older sitcoms relied on likable characters
and goofy hijinks, but Seinfeld, in contrast,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.220
famously had the unofficial motto of "no hugging,
no learning.”

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.260
In one of many tongue-in-cheek plots, we even
watched characters pitch a Seinfeld-esque

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.120
show to an NBC executive as "a show about
nothing."

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.440
“I think I can sum up the show for you with
one word: nothing.”

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.100
“Nothing?”

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.400
“Nothing!”

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.500
The show's tendency to make episodes out of
anything meant that viewers had no idea what

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.160
the show would focus on week to week.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.060
Viewers could jump in anywhere without getting
lost and the characters of Seinfeld would

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.240
never learn a lesson... even if they really
should.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.400
That all changed in the series finale.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:17.920
Looking up

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.300
The series finale starts off on a positive
note.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.200
Jerry and George seem to have finalized their
deal for the Seinfeld-esque show they're making

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.140
called Jerry.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.440
“He wants to sit down with us and talk about
Jerry.”

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.200
"Our show Jerry?”

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:32.840
“Right.”

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.200
“Jerry?

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.000
Oh my god he wants to talk about Jerry.”

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:36.500
"Ya!"

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.800
Network executives even loan the cast a private
jet, so Jerry and his pals decide to go to

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:42.300
Paris.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.060
It's about the happiest ending the self-obsessed
crew could get, so of course it doesn't last.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:51.610
Almost immediately, they muck things up by
nearly crashing the plane, and get stuck in

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.880
a small Massachusetts town while they wait
for it to get fixed.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.670
In classic Seinfeld fashion, they witness
a crime and crack jokes instead of helping

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.260
the victim.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.880
“Ah it’s a shame, alright I’m gonna
call NBC.”

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.140
However, the characters aren't in New York,
and their big city attitude lands them in

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.700
court for failing to help.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.940
They finally experience real consequences
for their daily rudeness almost as soon as

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:16.980
they leave New York.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.320
Karma for past deeds

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:24.630
Of course, it's ridiculous that they're arrested
for failing to help a carjacking victim, especially

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.190
when, as their lawyer reminds the judge, the
actual criminal is still out there.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.820
"So how can a bystander be guilty?

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:31.480
No such thing.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.100
Have you ever heard of a guilty bystander?”

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.900
Still, you can't say that Jerry, George, Elaine,
and Kramer didn't have it coming in some way.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.800
“So they just stood there and did nothing?”

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.380
“Yeah, nothing, nothing!”

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.500
The actual crime they get arrested for might
be "criminal indifference," but the real reason

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.720
that they get arrested is that they've amassed
so much negative karma over the years that

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:55.960
something needed to break.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.480
Even their acts of kindness are tainted with
selfishness, like when George tries to take

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.120
back a tip when the cashier doesn't notice
him originally tipping.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.660
Character witnesses

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.900
During their trial, a series of character
witnesses become a greatest hits parade of

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.730
some of the funniest Seinfeld episodes, while
also reminding viewers just how many awful

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.620
things the cast has done.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.400
“The idiots clowns did not know how to order...I
banned that one, the woman for a year.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.020
Then one day…she came back.”

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.500
Instead of taking the opportunity to realize
how many people they've hurt, Jerry, Elaine,

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.320
George, and Kramer seem largely unmoved.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.260
And then, they're judged...

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.860
“We find the defendants guilty!”

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.440
...and sentenced to a year in prison.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.360
Nothing ever ends

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.580
“Oh yeah, the second button is the key button,
it literally makes or breaks the shirt.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.320
Look at it, it's too high.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:56.820
It’s in no man’s land.”

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:02.360
After they get convicted, Jerry talks to George
about his shirt button placement, in an exact

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.830
rehash of the very first lines of Seinfeld.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.520
“See now to me that button's in the worst
possible spot.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.220
The second button literally makes or breaks
the shirt.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.980
Look at it, it's too high.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.380
It’s in no man’s land."

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.720
George responds by asking if they've had the
conversation before, and having completely

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:24.479
ignored any chance to redeem themselves, the
show ends exactly where it begins: with George

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.980
and Jerry having a conversation about nothing.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.060
Controversial conclusion

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:32.140
Love it or hate it, the ending is certainly
bold.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.540
Unfortunately, it was also pretty divisive.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:39.759
Entertainment Weekly gave it a C- at the time,
while USA Today published a scathing review

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:40.640
of the finale.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.900
On the other hand, Grantland published a defense
of the finale in 2015.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.800
It wasn't just critics who were torn either,
the actors were divided about the unique ending.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.500
Jerry Seinfeld himself initially defended
the show in a Reddit AMA, saying

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.840
"I was happy with the Seinfeld finale because
we didn't want to do another episode as much

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.000
as we wanted to have everybody come back to
the show we had so much fun with."

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:08.400
Julia Louis-Dreyfus, who played Elaine, even
made a joke about the finale on The Late Show

00:05:08.409 --> 00:05:09.499
with David Letterman.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:15.100
“Thanks for letting me take part in another
hugely disappointing series finale.”

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.100
Not the real ending?

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.900
While the actual finale of Seinfeld might
still be debated, it's not necessarily the

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.120
final word from co-creator Larry David.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:32.560
On Curb Your Enthusiasm, David put together
a Seinfeld finale more in keeping with fans'

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.689
expectations, when he brings the cast back
together in a ploy to regain the affections

00:05:36.689 --> 00:05:37.689
of his ex-wife.

00:05:37.689 --> 00:05:41.189
“Larry we already screwed up one finale,
we can’t do another.”

00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:43.260
“We didn’t screw up a finale, that was
a good finale!”

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:43.760
"Yaaa…."

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:48.320
In the reunion, Jerry has gotten together
with Elaine, while George tries to get back

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.560
together with his ex-wife Amanda after losing
all of his money in pyramid scheme.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.900
Jerry and Elaine have even had a daughter
together.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.460
The ending is much closer to what fans likely
pictured when Seinfeld was wrapping up its

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:03.970
run, and David even called the faux reunion,
quote, "a perfect way to do something like

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:05.279
that but not to do it."

